The space of plane conics is \(\mathbb P^{5}\).  
A point condition is linear (codimension 1), tangency to a fixed line is a quadratic
condition (degree 2), and tangency to a fixed smooth conic is a cubic condition
(degree 3).  

Hence for three given points \(P_{1},P_{2},P_{3}\) we intersect in \(\mathbb P^{5}\)

\[
\underbrace{H\;(\text{through }P_{1})}_{\deg 1}\;
\cap\;
\underbrace{H\;(\text{through }P_{2})}_{\deg 1}\;
\cap\;
\underbrace{H\;(\text{through }P_{3})}_{\deg 1}\;
\cap\;
\underbrace{Q\;(\text{tangent to }L)}_{\deg 2}\;
\cap\;
\underbrace{C\;(\text{tangent to }C_{0})}_{\deg 3}.
\]

The total degree of this zero‑dimensional intersection is  

\[
1\cdot 1\cdot 1\cdot 2\cdot 3 = 6 .
\]

Thus, for general choices of the three points, the line \(L\) and the conic
\(C_{0}\), there are exactly six conics satisfying the three point conditions,
being tangent to \(L\) and to \(C_{0}\).  
Since the configuration is generic, none of these conics is singular; all are
smooth.

\[
\boxed{6}
\]
